.SS "\fBsc_scda_fcontext_t\fP* sc_scda_fwrite_varray (\fBsc_scda_fcontext_t\fP * fc, const char * user_string, size_t * len, \fBsc_array_t\fP * array_data, \fBsc_array_t\fP * elem_counts, \fBsc_array_t\fP * elem_sizes, \fBsc_array_t\fP * proc_sizes, int indirect, int encode, \fBsc_scda_ferror_t\fP * errcode)"

.PP
Write a variable-size array file section\&. This is a collective function\&. This function can be used instead of \fBsc_scda_fwrite_array\fP if the array elements do not have a constant element size in bytes\&. 
.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
All parameters except of \fBarray_data\fP and \fBelem_sizes\fP are collective\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBWarning\fP
.RS 4
The API of this function will change in the next libsc version!
.RE
.PP
This function returns NULL on I/O errors\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIfc\fP File context previously opened by \fBsc_scda_fopen_write\fP\&. 
.br
\fIuser_string\fP At most \fBSC_SCDA_USER_STRING_BYTES\fP + 1 bytes in a nul-terminated string\&. See the 'User Strings' section in the detailed description of this file for more information\&. 
.br
\fIlen\fP The number of bytes in \fBuser_string\fP excluding the terminating nul\&. On NULL as input \fBuser_string\fP is expected to be a nul-terminated C string\&. 
.br
\fIarray_data\fP Let p be the calling rank\&. If \fBindirect\fP is false, \fBarray_data\fP must have element count 1 and as element size the p-th entry of \fBproc_sizes\fP\&. The data of the array must be the local array elements conforming with \fBelem_counts\fP, \fBproc_sizes\fP and \fBelem_sizes\fP\&. If \fBindirect\fP is true, \fBarray_data\fP must be a \fBsc_array\fP with element count equal to the p-th array entry of \fBelem_counts\fP and element size equal to sizeof (sc_array_t)\&. Each array element is again a \fBsc_array\fP\&. Now, with element count 1 and element size equals to the actual array element size as passed in \fBelem_sizes\fP\&. 
.br
\fIelem_counts\fP An \fBsc_array\fP that must be equal on all ranks\&. The element count of \fBelem_counts\fP must be the mpisize of the MPI communicator that was used to create \fBfc\fP\&. The element size of the \fBsc_array\fP must be equal to sizeof (unint8_t)\&. The \fBsc_array\fP must contain the local array elements counts\&. That is why it induces the partition that is used to write the array data in parallel\&. 
.br
\fIelem_sizes\fP A \fBsc_array\fP with the element sizes for the local array elements\&. The \fBsc_array\fP has an element count of p-th entry of \fBelem_counts\fP for p being the calling rank\&. The element size is sizeof (\fBsc_scda_ulong\fP)\&. 
.br
\fIproc_sizes\fP An \fBsc_array\fP that must be equal on all ranks\&. The element count and element size must be the same as for \fBelem_counts\fP\&. The array must contain the overall byte count per rank conforming with the passed array element partition \fBelem_counts\fP and the local array element sizes in \fBelem_sizes\fP\&. This parameter can be computed using \fBsc_scda_proc_sizes\fP\&. 
.br
\fIindirect\fP A Boolean to determine whether \fBarray_data\fP must be a \fBsc_array\fP of sc_arrays to write indirectly and in particular from potentially non-contigous memory\&. In the remaining case of \fBindirect\fP being false \fBarray_data\fP must be a \fBsc_array\fP with the actual array elements as data as further explained in the documentation of \fBarray_data\fP\&. 
.br
\fIencode\fP A Boolean to decide whether the file section is written compressed\&. This results in two written file sections that can be read without the encoding interpretation by using \fBsc_scda_fread_section_header\fP with decode set to false followed by the usual sc_scda_fread functions\&. The data can be read as passed to this function by using decode true in \fBsc_scda_fread_section_header\fP and calling the usual sc_scda_fread function\&. See also the 'Encoding' section in the detailed description in this file\&. 
.br
\fIerrcode\fP An errcode that can be interpreted by \fBsc_scda_ferror_string\fP or mapped to an error class by \fBsc_scda_ferror_class\fP\&. \fBerrcode\fP encodes success if and only if the function does not return NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
Return a pointer to the input context \fBfc\fP on success\&. The context is used to continue writing and eventually closing the file\&. In case of any error, attempt to close the file and deallocate the context \fBfc\fP\&. 
.RE
.PP

